[2026/05/14 14:33:58] [DEBUG] isgsa.backend/openai: [cyan]Raw func call response: Choice(finish_reason='tool_calls', index=0, logprobs=None, message=ChatCompletionMessage(content=None, refusal=None, role='assistant', annotations=[], audio=None, function_call=None, tool_calls=[ChatCompletionMessageFunctionToolCall(id='call_dHcgyYDrIvsV2pDVDiAiNHlB', function=Function(arguments='{"nl_text":"Plan: 1) Load the MovieLens 100k u.data file as a tab-delimited DataFrame with columns user, item, rating, timestamp. 2) Compute the total number of unique users and items to report dataset scale. 3) Find the top 3 most active users by rating count and compute the mean rating for the top user. 4) Calculate the co-occurrence of Item 50 and Item 181 by counting users who rated both items. 5) Inspect the timestamp range to detect any entries outside the 1997-1998 window and report any outliers. 6) Create a simple train/test split (80/20) and implement a baseline predictor using global mean plus per-user and per-item biases, clamped to [1,5]. 7) Evaluate the model with RMSE on the test set and print an epoch-like log message. 8) Collect all results in an experiment_data structure and save it as a NumPy file in the working directory for reproducibility. 9) While the goal is a solid baseline, we will rely on a straightforward, transparent approach that can be extended later, keeping the process fully reproducible. 10) The code will be self-contained and execute without requiring external hyperparameter tuning.","code":"import os\\nimport sys\\nimport numpy as np\\nimport pandas as pd\\nfrom datetime import datetime\\n\\n# Working directory setup as required\\nworking_dir = os.path.join(os.getcwd(), \'working\')\\nos.makedirs(working_dir, exist_ok=True)\\n\\n# Locate the MovieLens 100k u.data file\\ndata_path = \'u.data\'\\nif not os.path.exists(data_path):\\n    possible = [\\n        os.path.join(\'ml-100k\', \'u.data\'),\\n        os.path.join(\'ml-100k\', \'u.data.gz\'),\\n        os.path.join(\'data\', \'u.data\'),\\n    ]\\n    for p in possible:\\n        if os.path.exists(p):\\n            data_path = p\\n            break\\n\\n# Load data\\ncol_names = [\'user\', \'item\', \'rating\', \'timestamp\']\\ndf = pd.read_csv(data_path, sep=\'\\\\t\', header=None, names=col_names, engine=\'python\')\\n\\n# Basic counts\\nn_users = int(df[\'user\'].nunique())\\nn_items = int(df[\'item\'].nunique())\\nprint(f\'Total unique users: {n_users}\')\\nprint(f\'Total unique items: {n_items}\')\\n\\n# Top 3 active users by rating count\\nuser_counts = df[\'user\'].value_counts()\\ntop3 = user_counts.head(3)\\nprint(\\"Top 3 active users (by rating count):\\")\\nprint(top3.to_string())\\nif not top3.empty:\\n    top1_user = top3.index[0]\\n    mean_top1 = df.loc[df[\'user\'] == top1_user, \'rating\'].mean()\\nelse:\\n    top1_user = None\\n    mean_top1 = None\\nprint(f\'Top1 user: {top1_user}, mean rating: {mean_top1}\')\\n\\n# Co-occurrence for items 50 and 181\\nusers_50 = set(df.loc[df[\'item\'] == 50, \'user\'].unique())\\nusers_181 = set(df.loc[df[\'item\'] == 181, \'user\'].unique())\\nco_occurrence = len(users_50 & users_181)\\nprint(f\'Number of users who rated both item 50 and item 181: {co_occurrence}\')\\n\\n# Timestamp range check (1997-1998 window)\\nts_min = int(df[\'timestamp\'].min())\\nts_max = int(df[\'timestamp\'].max())\\nmin_date = pd.to_datetime(ts_min, unit=\'s\')\\nmax_date = pd.to_datetime(ts_max, unit=\'s\')\\nrange_start = pd.Timestamp(year=1997, month=1, day=1)\\nrange_end = pd.Timestamp(year=1998, month=12, day=31)\\nout_of_range = (min_date < range_start) or (max_date > range_end)\\nprint(f\'CSV timestamp range: min={min_date}, max={max_date}, outside_range={out_of_range}\')\\n\\n# Prepare a simple train/test split (80/20) for a baseline\\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\\ntrain_df, test_df = train_test_split(df, test_size=0.2, random_state=42)\\n\\n# Baseline: global mean + user bias + item bias\\nglobal_mean = train_df[\'rating\'].mean()\\nuser_bias = train_df.groupby(\'user\')[\'rating\'].mean() - global_mean\\nitem_bias = train_df.groupby(\'item\')[\'rating\'].mean() - global_mean\\n\\ndef predict(row):\\n    u = row[\'user\']\\n    i = row[\'item\']\\n    bu = float(user_bias.get(u, 0.0))\\n    bi = float(item_bias.get(i, 0.0))\\n    pred = global_mean + bu + bi\\n    if pred < 1.0:\\n        pred = 1.0\\n    if pred > 5.0:\\n        pred = 5.0\\n    return pred\\n\\n# Evaluate on test set\\ntest_preds = test_df.apply(predict, axis=1)\\ntest_rmse = float(np.sqrt(np.mean((test_df[\'rating\'].values - test_preds.values) ** 2)))\\nprint(f\'Epoch 0: validation_loss = {test_rmse:.4f}\')\\n\\n# Train RMSE (on train data, using same model)\\ntrain_preds = train_df.apply(predict, axis=1)\\ntrain_rmse = float(np.sqrt(np.mean((train_df[\'rating\'].values - train_preds.values) ** 2)))\\n\\n# Prepare experiment data structure for saving\\nexperiment_data = {\\n    \'movielens_100k_baseline\': {\\n        \'metrics\': {\\n            \'train\': [train_rmse],\\n            \'val\': [test_rmse],\\n        },\\n        \'losses\': {\\n            \'train\': [],\\n            \'val\': [],\\n        },\\n        \'predictions\': test_preds.to_numpy(),\\n        \'ground_truth\': test_df[\'rating\'].to_numpy(),\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n# Save summary to disk\\nnp.save(os.path.join(working_dir, \'experiment_data.npy\'), experiment_data)\\n"}', name='return_plan_and_code'), type='function')]))[/cyan]
